A||||10||ChadMan||"Get a reliable car"...ha||||1
Z||000000||ChadMan||10-23-1999||06:13 PM||||This college-aged guy I work with was interested in my 86 SE V6 that I'm selling, but his father (a mechanic) told him, "If you buy that piece of $hit, you're an idiot."<P>Then the guy goes on to tell me about the experience he's had with his 1997 Dodge Avenger:  transmission problems, brake problems, and all sorts of electrical problems.<P>I didn't have the heart to tell him that the SE has broken down a grand total of ZERO times since I purchased it a year ago.  You'd expect a brand new car to be more reliable than a 14-year-old Pontiac with 122K miles, right?  But hey, as their new commercials remind us:  Dodge is different!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||10-23-1999||06:32 PM||||It's (unfortunately) a common reaction amongst a lot of mechanists. The Fiero is special in many ways which leaves a lot of mechanics baffled if they're trying to fix something on it. And rather than admitting they do not understand the Fiero, they'll say it's a piece of junk. I've encountered it many times here in Holland, where the Fiero is indeed a rare sight.<P>The Fiero was ofcourse way ahead of its time, both in design and the way the engine is controlled by the Engine Control Module. The Fiero is like most modern cars are today - you need special tools and equipment to work on it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||10-23-1999||06:49 PM||||At a computer workshop on Wednesday, I got bored with the lecture and started surfing the web. Of course, I came here. A bystander commented on my waste of time and money on my Fiero but went on to say that the fuel pump on his truck had costed him over $400 to have replaced and it needed new injectors too- on a 95 model S-10 truck! I just smiled and said that it must be nice to have that much money to waste on such a reliable vehicle.||||reg||8
Z||000003||trackerrrr||10-24-1999||12:31 AM||||My wife and I just got our first Fiero, a 1984 Sport Coupe.  I too have been told that Fieros were bad cars, fire prone, etc.  Well, this car has 309,000 miles on it and is still going strong!  When I got it it wouldn't pull up a hill very well.  I spent $15(!) on new plugs and wires and now it's a screamer!  The only other thing I've replaced is a hub assembly ($50).  In the 3 1/2 weeks we've put almost 3000 miles on it traveling from California to New Mexico to Oklahoma and back.  We didn't have so much as an ounce of trouble.  We got the car from the original owner and he was very meticulous about regular maintenance.  It still gets 30 mpg on the highway too!  Fieros are great!!!||||reg||14
Z||000004||fieroshop||10-24-1999||10:09 AM||||If you guys have seen a lot try this for size. A guy finds me on the internet and calls to say that his car is in the shop and the shop is saying that a lot of things could probably be wrong starting with a bad water pump and the minnimum that it would cost is $400.00 and that they would do it but prefered not to. So I call the shop to arrange for pickup wit my trailer since I tow free of charge to my shop and the owner tells me to bring him a few cards so that he could pass them on to his frreinds. That has happened several times now including a shop that put a new engine in an 85 GT and it has a cracked head and would I install a new motor and that they would pay for it not his cusctomer who of course will be my customer now.Oh well! thats life||||reg||10
Z||000005||DJRice||10-25-1999||08:59 AM||||Its kind of silly the way mechanics act when the see a Fiero pulling up.  I mean, The car is not really that much more complicated the n your average late model front wheel drive car.  Either way, things are packed in so tight that bloody knuckles are a given (at least for someone my size).<P>When I first moved to where I am now the water pump died in my 87 and I had no way of taking care of it at the time, so I had to pay a mechanic.  After the work was done, the counter guy said, "The guys really hate seeing those Fiero's come in, but we got the job done."  Of course, after the car overheated because Cletus and Billy Joe did not know how to properly refill the cooling system, I went back and had a talk with the store manager.  And I fixed the problem myself because I didnt want them touching my car again.  Now I know one mechanic to cross out in my phone book next time I need repairs done.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||10-25-1999||01:11 PM||||Yep,  I checked the compression on my 86 SE V6 with 188K miles on it prior to taking it in to get smogged (finally passed), and was amazed to see 138-148 PSI across all six cylinders.  Very good for a nearly 14 year old engine.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bill Osmer||10-25-1999||01:53 PM||||I bought my 86 GT about 3 months ago. When I drove it home, I noticed the charging system wasn't. So the 1st weekend was spent finding out how to change the alternator ($29.95). Then the waterpump went out - another weekend ($24.95). Then I had new brakes put on (by a local shop) to the tune of $600 (OUCH!!). Since then, I've replaced the E-brake cable ($14.95), changed the exhaust manifolds (used manifolds: $100 ea, new gaskets {the real problem} $19.95) & sparkplugs ($15) and replaced the headliner ($5 for matl. & $12 for adhesive). <P>Except for the brakes, alternator and waterpump, I knew about the other problems. Now, the car is sound and dependable. BTW, I drive it daily with no worries about repairs I do myself - just others (  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">) I have no problems believing that this car is dependable - just like ANY USED CAR, one has to do maintenance.<P>[This message has been edited by Bill Osmer (edited 10-25-1999).]<p>[This message has been edited by Bill Osmer (edited 10-25-1999).]||||reg||6
Z||000008||ChadMan||10-26-1999||09:06 AM||||You got an alternator for $30!?!  You da man, Bill.  I paid $80 at NAPA.  Oh well.||||reg||8
Z||000009||sdm1964||10-26-1999||08:09 PM||||I just bought an 85 GT three weeks ago to replace my camaro Z28 that was wrecked.I high school kid pulled out in front of me after 2 years of work. New paint, carpet, suspension etc. As I weep over the bent front end i climb into that fine GT that i stole for $1600.00 from some guy in south carolina. Everything works, new paint interior etc. The Z will be back but i am in no hurry now that i have this fine little pocket rocket. I am now thinking about trading the Z for an 86 or above GT, love the lines.||||reg||6
Z||000010||DJRice||10-27-1999||09:04 AM||||You know, if the engine didnt get ruined in the Z28, and you found another fiero that you didnt need for a daily driver........<P>Well, you get the picture.||||reg||1
